  "live_records": {
    "q-73139": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73139",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14223189/assets/17f6b2ab535408802ef2776eea2db41ac20b17f3568e245ee87b13f05767203f.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}$ between $x=0$, $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?",
      "worked_solution": "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3-1}f(0+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}f(k) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}\\sqrt{9-{k}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
