      "source_example_id": "e-401",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing a Left Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation",
      "kp_id": "e-401"
    },
    "q-345028": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345028",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14223189/assets/7d7a51344b945f502ace0aa7b9d5dee46514a465adda7666d83fe91bd2ffb3a2.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ between $x=0$, $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $k.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3}$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=4$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=4,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-0}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{4-1}f(0+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3}f(k) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
